00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:52,580
This is the story of Planet 9,

17
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:54,540
a world nobody has ever seen.

18
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:56,620
The scientists who are sure it's there,

19
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:58,020
the ones who think they're wrong,

20
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:02,580
and the telescope that just started the search that might finally decide it.

21
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:08,400
Chapter 1, The Man Who Killed Pluto In October of 2003,

22
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:13,360
an astronomer named Mike Brown was using a telescope at Palomar Observatory in California,

23
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,780
looking for objects far beyond Neptune.

24
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:19,440
What he found was a body out past Pluto,

25
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,460
almost exactly the same size.

26
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:25,260
He named it Eris, after the Greek goddess of strife and discord.

27
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:27,160
The name turned out to be fitting.

28
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:30,560
If Eris was really a planet's twin,

29
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:32,600
then either Eris was the 10th planet,

30
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:34,660
or Pluto had never really been one.

31
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:36,880
Astronomers couldn't have it both ways anymore.

32
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:43,500
On August 24, 2006, the International Astronomical Union met in

33
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:47,200
Prague and voted. Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet.

34
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:50,860
Mike Brown became, quite literally,

35
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:52,420
the man who killed Pluto.

36
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:54,880
He later wrote a real memoir about it,

37
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:59,300
with the actual title, How I Killed Pluto and Why It Had It Coming.

38
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,840
A decade later, the same man would be the one telling the world there was a real,

39
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:07,900
undiscovered planet hiding at the edge of the solar system.

40
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:14,020
Chapter 2, The Pattern That Shouldn't Exist In 2014,

41
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:17,360
two other astronomers, Chad Trujillo and Scott Shepard,

42
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:22,940
discovered a distant icy object called 2012 VP113.

43
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:26,180
They noticed something strange.

44
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:29,080
That object, and a dozen others like it,

45
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:32,520
all had orbits that pointed in a suspiciously similar direction.

46
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:33,960
They couldn't explain why,

47
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:38,720
they just flagged it. Mike Brown and a younger colleague at Caltech,

48
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,140
named Konstantin Batigan,

49
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:43,360
took a closer look. They found six of the most distant,

50
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:45,500
best -measured objects in that group.

51
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:48,640
All six shared something specific,

52
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,440
the point in their orbit where they swing closest to the sun,

53
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:53,400
all clustered in nearly the same direction.

54
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:56,200
And their orbital planes were all tilted the same way,

55
00:02:56,260 --> 00:03:01,640
too. Batigan and Brown calculated the odds of that happening by pure random

56
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:05,560
chance. Their answer? Roughly one in 15 ,000.

57
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:09,540
There was one explanation that fit cleanly.

58
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:12,660
A real, massive, undiscovered planet.

59
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:16,480
Its own gravity herding all six objects into that same pattern.

60
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,920
On January 20th, 2016, Caltech announced it publicly.

61
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:26,460
Mike Brown's own words, This would be a real ninth planet.

62
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:30,360
There have only been two true planets discovered since ancient times,

63
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:31,800
and this would be a third.

64
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:35,440
Konstantin Batigan put it even more plainly.

65
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:38,480
For the first time in over 150 years,

66
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:42,540
there is solid evidence that the Solar System's planetary census is incomplete.

67
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:47,040
Chapter 3. A world we've never seen.

68
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:50,880
If it's real, what exactly is out there?

69
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:55,460
The 2016 paper estimated something more than ten times the mass of Earth.

70
00:03:56,820 --> 00:03:59,220
A later refinement, published in 2021,

71
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:02,360
narrowed that down to about six times Earth's mass.

72
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:05,640
Something like a small, distant cousin of Neptune.

73
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:09,020
Its orbit would be nothing like ours.

74
00:04:09,020 --> 00:04:13,320
On average, about 300 times farther from the Sun than Earth is,

75
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:17,980
on a path so long it would take somewhere between 10 and 20 ,000 years to complete a

76
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:19,220
single trip around the Sun.

77
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:24,920
For comparison, the last ice age ended about 12 ,000 years ago.

78
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:29,160
Planet 9, if it's real, hasn't even finished one lap since then.

79
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:33,080
It would be so far away, and so cold,

80
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:37,300
that no telescope has ever caught even a single point of reflected light from it.

81
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:40,220
Every image you've ever seen of it is not real.

82
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:44,120
It's an artist's guess. Chapter 4.

83
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:47,900
The 10 -year hunt. Since 2016,

84
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:51,420
Brown and Batigan's own search has leaned heavily on one instrument,

85
00:04:51,860 --> 00:04:55,100
the Subaru telescope on the summit of Mauna Kea in Hawaii.

86
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:58,480
Other teams joined in with their own surveys,

87
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:02,940
the Dark Energy Survey, an archive search using the Zwicky transient facility,

88
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:05,920
even old infrared data going back decades.

89
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:10,000
A decade of searching has not found Planet 9.

90
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:14,940
It has, however, found more objects like the ones that started this whole idea.

91
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:20,340
In July of 2025, the Subaru team announced a real discovery,

92
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:22,720
a new object nicknamed Ammonite,

93
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:25,280
only the fourth of its kind ever confirmed.

94
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:30,480
Here's the complication. Ammonite's orbit does not line up with the others.

95
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:33,620
If anything, it weakens the original pattern,

96
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:37,760
not strengthens it. The discovery's lead researcher,

97
00:05:38,140 --> 00:05:39,540
Yukun Huang, said it plainly.

98
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:44,200
The fact that Ammonite's current orbit does not align with those of the other three Sednoids

99
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:46,600
lowers the likelihood of the Planet 9 hypothesis.

100
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:51,200
Ten years of looking had added a new piece of evidence,

101
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:55,100
and that new piece cut against the theory it was supposed to help confirm.

102
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:59,040
Chapter 5. The scientists who think they're wrong.

103
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:02,880
Not every astronomer accepts the Planet 9 explanation.

104
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:05,800
Some think the entire pattern might be an illusion.

105
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:09,820
Their argument is about where astronomers happen to be looking,

106
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:11,320
not what's actually out there.

107
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:15,800
These objects are only bright enough to spot during one narrow part of a millennia -long

108
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:20,280
orbit. Ground telescopes don't scan the whole sky evenly.

109
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,180
They favour certain seasons,

110
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:24,600
certain patches, certain depths.

111
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:29,740
A University of Michigan researcher named Kevin Napier ran the numbers a different way,

112
00:06:29,980 --> 00:06:31,740
correcting for exactly that bias.

113
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:37,480
His result, once you account for where and when astronomers were actually searching,

114
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,240
the supposed alignment disappears completely.

115
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:44,100
Statistically, it looked like nothing more than random chance.

116
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:49,120
Brown and Battingen disagree and have published their own rebuttal.

117
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:54,340
This is not settled. Both sides are still actively publishing papers against each other.

118
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:58,380
There are other explanations that need no hidden planet at all.

119
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:03,880
One astronomer proposed that a disk of many small icy bodies acting together could fake

120
00:07:03,880 --> 00:07:06,560
the same pattern through their own combined gravity.

121
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:10,900
A University of Washington astronomer named Pedro Bernardinelli,

122
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:13,220
who works directly on this kind of survey,

123
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:16,480
put the real state of the debate about as honestly as anyone has.

124
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:21,120
The agnostic perspective now is that we do not have enough data either way.

125
00:07:22,820 --> 00:07:24,140
Mike Brown, for his part,

126
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:27,560
hasn't backed down. As recently as 2024,

127
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:32,340
he said plainly, I think it is very unlikely that P9 does not exist.

128
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:39,200
Two respected scientists looking at the same handful of objects reaching opposite conclusions.

129
00:07:39,860 --> 00:07:42,420
That's not a scandal. That's just what real,

130
00:07:42,860 --> 00:07:44,560
unfinished science looks like.

131
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:48,140
Chapter 6. It keeps getting stranger.

132
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:52,380
Even Mike Brown doesn't chase every candidate that comes along.

133
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:58,280
In 2025, a separate team combed 40 -year -old infrared satellite data and

134
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:00,660
found a possible signal far out in the dark.

135
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:03,820
Brown's public response was blunt.

136
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:05,480
Whether it is real or not,

137
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:07,840
it is 100 % not Planet 9.

138
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:11,120
The orbit simply didn't match the pattern his own theory predicted.

139
00:08:12,240 --> 00:08:17,340
Then, in August of 2025, a Princeton team proposed something else entirely.

140
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:20,620
A second, separate, smaller,

141
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:24,720
hidden world closer in, based on a completely different piece of evidence.

142
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:27,940
They're calling that one Planet Y.

143
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:30,600
It's not a rival explanation for the same objects.

144
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:35,660
It's a completely separate mystery hiding in a completely different part of the data.

145
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:39,200
Another team modeled something stranger still,

146
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:42,880
a small chance that our solar system didn't grow this planet at all,

147
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:47,920
but simply captured a rogue world drifting through the galaxy billions of years ago.

148
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:53,140
Ten years in, this isn't one clean mystery with one clean answer waiting.

149
00:08:53,480 --> 00:08:54,740
It's gotten more complicated,

150
00:08:54,980 --> 00:08:59,360
not less. Chapter 7. The machine that Mike decided.

151
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:04,560
For a decade, the honest answer to does Planet 9 exist has been,

152
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:06,260
nobody knows for certain.

153
00:09:06,260 --> 00:09:08,660
That may finally be about to change.

154
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:12,360
The Vera Sea, Reuben Observatory,

155
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:14,460
built high in the mountains of Chile,

156
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:19,760
is designed to photograph the entire visible sky over and over every few

157
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:21,760
nights for 10 straight years.

158
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:27,980
On June 30, 2026, its main 10 -year survey officially began.

159
00:09:28,740 --> 00:09:32,400
Weeks later, its first real batch of data started reaching astronomers.

160
00:09:33,960 --> 00:09:36,640
One veteran of this exact kind of search,

161
00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:38,200
astronomer Scott Shepard,

162
00:09:38,380 --> 00:09:43,320
put real odds on it. If Planet 9 is out there the way Brown and Batigan describe it,

163
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:47,620
Reuben has roughly a 70 -80 % chance of actually finding it.

164
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:49,880
Konstantin Batigan himself,

165
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:52,340
speaking just weeks before this was filmed,

166
00:09:52,580 --> 00:09:54,580
said it as directly as a scientist can,

167
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:58,720
we will get that new batch of discoveries basically starting this summer,

168
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:03,900
starting summer 2026. It will be a direct test of all lines of evidence.

169
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:08,000
As of right now, no announcement has been made,

170
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:10,040
no detection, no exclusion.

171
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:13,300
The search is simply finally actually running.

172
00:10:14,660 --> 00:10:19,160
What stays with me about this story isn't whether Planet 9 turns out to be real.

173
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:22,440
It's that a handful of frozen rocks,

174
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:24,860
barely visible, even to our best telescopes,

175
00:10:25,140 --> 00:10:28,980
were enough to make some of the smartest people alive seriously ask whether we've been

176
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:30,300
missing an entire planet.

177
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:35,540
And it's that real science doesn't need the mystery solved to be worth following.

178
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,120
A decade of disagreement,

179
00:10:37,660 --> 00:10:39,900
biased corrections, dead -end candidates,

180
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:42,340
and rival theories is not a failure.

181
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:46,220
It's just what looking for the truth actually looks like in real time.

182
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:48,740
Somewhere in Chile tonight,

183
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,320
a telescope built for exactly this question is scanning the sky again.

184
00:10:52,840 --> 00:10:54,220
Nobody knows yet what it will find.

185
00:10:55,540 --> 00:10:58,900
If a planet nobody has ever seen doesn't sound strange enough,

186
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:02,880
wait until you hear the story of a machine NASA built to last five years.

187
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:07,540
That's still flying 49 years later and is only now starting to go dark.

